    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. One thing I'm worried about is the sheer amount of uncatalogued material we're inheriting. A lot of these manuscripts are currently just in crates, totally unsorted and physically disorganized. If we pick a system that's too rigid, I'm afraid the process of trying to impose order on that initial chaos might become overwhelming for my staff. Do you have any advice on how these two systems handle the transition from a completely unorganized state to a structured one?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For transitioning from a completely unorganized state to a structured one, a classification system's flexibility and the simplicity of the rules for moving materials from unsorted piles into the categories can be critical. DDC, with its somewhat intuitive approach, might seem more approachable at an initial sorting stage. It would allow your staff to start placing items into broader categories without getting bogged down in the specifics, which could make the mountain of uncatalogued material seem more manageable. On the other hand, LCC's detailed system might seem daunting, but it also provides more precise guidance for handling difficult-to-classify items. This precision can be invaluable for finding an appropriate home for manuscripts that don't fit easily into general classifications, but it may require more training to navigate the complexity.\n\nIt's also worth noting that both systems allow for subject-specific cross-referencing within their classification; this could be a helpful tool when determining where the manuscripts fit within the broader landscape of knowledge. It will be important to consider the expertise of your staff, their willingness to take on the steep learning curve associated with LCC, and whether additional training could be feasibly applied to facilitate the transition from chaos to order within your collection.",
      "source": "target",
